# SEQUENCE 11 - WHAT HE LEFT

FADE IN:

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - MAINTENANCE ROOM - LATER

The two records remain open on the worktable.

Outside the slatted windows --

Children chase each other through the yard.

A radio changes stations.

Someone laughs hard enough to cough.

Life continuing five feet away.

Malik has made three stacks.

COMPLETED.

REPEATING.

UNREAD.

Ade watches him make a fourth.

ADE
What's that one?

MALIK
Trips.

ADE
Of course it is.

Passports. Receipts. Folded itineraries. Photocopied entry stamps tucked into ledgers.

Malik matches one trip to a maintenance date.

Then another.

Kai sits on the floor beneath the window with his aunt's notebooks around him.

He is not reading them straight through.

Inside covers.

Back pages.

Loose inserts.

Anything addressed to someone.

Nothing.

Malik writes a date on scrap paper.

MALIK
Six times in four years.

KAI
Seven.

Kai holds up an old boarding stub from inside one of the notebooks.

KAI
This one isn't in his ledger.

Malik takes it.

Adds the date.

Ade studies the two of them working opposite ends of the same dead man.

ADE
This is a terrible family vacation.

Nothing.

He lets the joke go.

Malik finds a ruled index.

BINDING MATERIALS.
BRASS.
WESTERN STONES.
RIVER LEVEL.
INNER FRAME.

He turns pages.

Stops.

A maintenance table.

Intervals measured in weeks and months.

Corrections in Solomon's hand.

The final completed line predates his death.

Below it --

future intervals.

Already ruled.

Already calculated.

Malik traces the next one.

MALIK
This is late.

Kai rises.

KAI
How late?

Malik checks the date again.

MALIK
Nine days.

The yard beyond the windows keeps going.

Kai looks toward the inner door.

KAI
Before we got here.

MALIK
After he died.

Kai studies the rows beneath it.

More dates.

KAI
He kept going.

Malik turns the page.

Materials.

Quantities.

Storage locations.

Key numbers.

Kai turns another page.

Blank.

Another.

Blank.

He checks the back cover.

Nothing.

KAI
Who does it?

Malik looks at him.

Kai flips to the index again.

KAI
There's no name.

He opens a drawer.

Red cotton bundles marked by weight.

KAI
Cloth.

Another drawer.

Keys with numbers stamped into paper tags.

KAI
Keys.

He finds a measurement sheet.

KAI
Three millimeters on a stone.

He looks at Malik.

KAI
But not a person.

Malik takes the half-open drawer before Kai can yank it farther.

MALIK
Slow down.

KAI
Where is the part for when he's dead?

MALIK
We haven't read everything.

KAI
He knew.

MALIK
Kai.

KAI
He knew he was dying.

Malik closes the drawer.

MALIK
I heard you.

KAI
Then stop looking for the folder he forgot.

Malik holds his stare.

KAI
He didn't forget it.

Malik returns to the schedule.

MALIK
Something was due nine days ago.

KAI
By who?

MALIK
I don't know.

KAI
Exactly.

Ade reaches over.

Closes Solomon's ledger.

Then closes the sister's notebook.

Both brothers look at him.

ADE
You're both finishing his sentence for him.

MALIK
I'm reading dates.

ADE
Then read the date.

To Kai --

ADE
And you read the blank page.

He puts the two closed books beside each other.

ADE
Neither one gets to tell us what Dad meant.

No one answers.

At the doorway --

Bode.

Behind him, Eno carries bread, fruit and water.

ENO
You have been in here long enough for breakfast to become lunch.

She sets the tray directly across Malik's stacks.

Malik moves the nearest papers away from the condensation.

Eno notices.

ENO
Good. Your hands still work.

She points at the food.

ENO
Use them.

Kai stays standing.

ENO
Kai.

He takes bread.

Bode enters.

Malik opens the ledger to the schedule.

MALIK
Did he talk about these dates?

Bode reads.

BODE
No.

MALIK
Ask you to continue them?

BODE
No.

KAI
Anyone?

Bode thinks.

BODE
Not that I know.

Malik taps the missed interval.

MALIK
What happens when it gets missed?

BODE
I don't know.

Kai absorbs the answer.

Eno hands him water.

KAI
He really left nothing.

Malik looks around the room.

MALIK
He left this.

KAI
That is not an instruction.

MALIK
I know.

Kai has no reply.

Eno looks from one brother to the other.

ENO
Whatever Solomon wanted, he is not here to do it.

She points through the open outer door toward the living house.

ENO
You are.

Malik looks at the schedule.

Then the inner door.

Then Kai.

MALIK
We don't touch the shrine.

KAI
And the brass?

MALIK
Stays where it is.

KAI
The inner door?

MALIK
No.

KAI
And if waiting is the problem?

MALIK
Then we find out what nine days ago was before we make another decision blind.

Kai looks at the missed date.

Then at Malik.

Ade takes the scrap with the missed date from Malik's hand.

Malik looks at him.

ADE
Not yours.

He puts it in the middle of the table.

ADE
Nobody goes anywhere alone.

Kai looks at him.

ADE
Nobody decides alone.

Malik considers the wording.

Then nods.

Kai takes longer.

Nods once.

Bode stays out of it.

Eno pushes the fruit toward Ade.

ENO
Now that democracy has finished, eat.

Ade takes a piece.

ADE
I preferred tyranny. Less paperwork.

A small release.

Malik almost gives him a smile.

Kai looks toward the inner door.

The loose brass fastener remains on the sill.

Exactly where it was.

He leaves it there.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE 12 - WHEN IT STAYS

EXT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - COURTYARD - NIGHT

The house is alive again.

Dinner aftermath.

Plastic chairs.

Children underfoot.

A football match on television inside draws periodic SHOUTS.

Generator vibration beneath everything.

Mosquitoes worry bare ankles.

Eno directs plates from one hand to another without appearing to move.

Ade dries dishes beside the kitchen door.

ADE
I crossed an ocean to become unpaid labor.

ENO
You were unemployed in my kitchen. I corrected it.

Ade goes back to the towel.

Kai sits near the courtyard door with his camera resting in his lap.

He is not looking through it.

A LITTLE GIRL, six, draws on the back of an old church program beside him.

She shows him a house with five windows.

LITTLE GIRL
This one is yours.

KAI
Why is mine small?

LITTLE GIRL
You are far.

Kai smiles.

Then --

METAL in his mouth.

He swallows.

It gets stronger.

His fingers tighten around the camera.

Across the courtyard, Malik sees his face change.

MALIK
Kai?

Kai looks at him.

KAI
Don't --

Pressure seals his ears.

The football crowd stretches into a thin electrical whine.

Then -- nothing.

The television still plays.

Men inside still react.

A cheer reaches Kai after the players have already reset.

He stands.

His body pitches hard left.

The courtyard does not.

He catches the chair.

Malik is already moving.

MALIK
Look at me.

Kai tries.

Malik's face will not become one face.

Two images overlap by half an inch.

Separate.

Rejoin.

KAI
It's here.

Ade sets the plate down.

The LITTLE GIRL looks up.

LITTLE GIRL
What is?

Ade crouches.

ADE
Your uncle forgot dinner exists.

He gives her the dish towel.

ADE
Take this to Auntie Eno. Ask for biscuits before she changes her mind.

LITTLE GIRL
She didn't say yes.

ADE
That's why we move quickly.

The girl runs toward the kitchen.

Ade rises.

His balance drops out from under him.

He catches the table.

His palm stops six inches before the edge his eyes insist is there.

He freezes.

ADE
Malik.

No joke now.

The GENERATOR HUM is gone.

The bulb above them burns steadily.

No mosquitoes circle it.

Kai looks toward the passage.

MALIK
No.

Kai doesn't turn.

MALIK
Kai. Me.

Kai tries.

The passage behind Malik is empty.

STUTTER.

Empty passage.

And something impossibly tall inside it.

No transition between the two.

Kai's knees strike tile.

He never feels himself fall.

His camera cracks against the floor.

MALIK
Kai!

Malik lunges.

STUTTER.

He is already beside him.

Momentum arrives afterward and nearly throws him past Kai.

Malik tries to inhale.

Nothing starts.

His chest is ready.

His body does not remember the next step.

He forces air out.

Drags one breath in.

Again.

Again.

Ade takes one step toward them.

His inner ear rotates the courtyard ninety degrees.

He drops to one hand.

A WOMAN carrying plates walks past him normally.

WOMAN
Ade?

He gets up before she can follow his eyes.

ADE
I'm fine.

He takes the plates from her.

ADE
Kitchen.

She goes.

Kai is staring past Malik.

MALIK
Don't.

Kai's eyes flood.

A red capillary spreads across the white of his right eye.

STUTTER.

The passage tries to contain the Entity.

Its height has nowhere to go.

One articulation folds beneath the ceiling.

Another begins behind plaster and finishes in open air.

STUTTER.

The arrangement changes.

The head occupies a position the neck cannot support.

STUTTER.

Closer.

No step.

The wooden frame CREAKS.

Dust lifts from the lintel --

hangs above it --

falls.

Kai's nose begins to bleed.

MALIK
Kai. Stop looking.

Kai tries to answer.

His tongue has gone numb.

KAI
I ca --

His mouth floods with saliva.

He turns and vomits onto the tile.

STUTTER.

A surface where a face might be tries to acquire depth.

STUTTER.

Almost human arrangement.

STUTTER.

Gone.

STUTTER.

There again.

The wall doubles.

The doorframe doubles.

Malik doubles.

Kai doubles.

The Entity does not.

Ade sees enough.

A high tone drills through his ears.

Then --

LITTLE GIRL (O.S.)
Uncle Kai?

Ade turns.

The girl stands at the kitchen threshold with two biscuits.

LITTLE GIRL
Why are you calling me?

Kai is on his knees vomiting.

His mouth is empty.

The girl looks past Ade toward the passage.

ADE
Hey.

She looks at him.

ADE
Those are mine.

LITTLE GIRL
Auntie said one is his.

ADE
She lied.

The girl laughs.

Ade reaches her.

Blocks the passage with his body.

Behind him --

STUTTER.

The girl's eyes start around his shoulder.

Ade lifts her.

ADE
Nope.

He takes one biscuit with his teeth.

ADE
Tax.

She protests into his shoulder as he carries her toward the courtyard.

Ade does not look back.

Blood begins running from his nose.

The girl touches his face.

LITTLE GIRL
You're bleeding.

ADE
I know.

At the passage --

Malik grips Kai's face with both hands.

MALIK
Me.

Kai's eyes keep pulling sideways.

MALIK
Kai.

STUTTER.

Malik's hands are on him --

and Kai is beneath the passage arch six feet away.

STUTTER.

Back in Malik's hands.

Kai SCREAMS.

His right arm goes dead.

It drops against his body.

MALIK
Can you move it?

Kai tries.

Nothing.

Behind Malik --

one unresolved articulation occupies the same apparent space as Kai's shoulder.

No strike.

No impact.

Kai convulses.

Sensation returns all at once.

His fingers claw the tile.

Malik starts to turn.

KAI
DON'T.

Malik stops.

Inside the house --

a chair scrapes.

ENO (O.S.)
What happened?

BODE (O.S.)
Malik?

Footsteps approaching.

Kai hears them.

KAI
Get them out.

MALIK
Ade!

Ade already has the girl outside.

Eno and Bode appear at the kitchen entrance.

Ade steps into their path.

ENO
Move.

ADE
Gas.

ENO
What?

ADE
I smelled gas. Everybody outside.

Eno looks past him.

Kai retches.

Malik is on the floor beside him.

ENO
Ade --

ADE
Please.

She sees the blood beneath his nose.

Turns immediately.

ENO
Everybody outside.

Complaints.

Questions.

ENO
Now.

People move.

A child is carried away from the passage.

A chair turns its back to it.

The men abandon the television.

The wooden passage frame stops creaking.

Malik gets a breath without forcing it.

Kai's double vision begins to settle.

STUTTER.

Entity.

STUTTER.

Empty passage.

STUTTER.

Something impossibly far behind the wall.

Then --

nothing.

Sound SLAMS back.

Generator.

Television crowd.

A baby crying.

Plates outside.

Insects.

Malik flinches from ordinary sound.

Kai curls around his right arm.

Ade stands in the courtyard holding the girl while blood reaches his lip.

LITTLE GIRL
Was it gas?

Ade looks at his brothers.

ADE
Yeah.

LITTLE GIRL
I didn't smell it.

ADE
Good.

He sets her beside Eno.

Eno takes in the three brothers.

Kai's bloodshot eye.

Malik breathing through his nose like he is counting each one.

Ade wiping blood with the back of his wrist.

ENO
Car.

Malik looks at Kai's arm.

MALIK
Fingers.

Kai moves them.

Slow.

MALIK
Again.

Kai does.

The tremor remains.

ENO
Malik.

MALIK
One minute.

ENO
You have one.

Bode is behind her.

Pale.

He keeps his eyes on the brothers.

Not the passage.

Kai tries to stand.

His arm trembles violently.

Ade reaches him first.

Kai lets him.

Malik gets under the other side.

The three move together without deciding who carries what.

At the passage entrance --

Kai stops.

MALIK
What?

Kai looks toward the open maintenance room.

Not into the passage darkness.

The INNER DOOR is visible beyond the outer threshold.

Near its lower hinge --

the brass fastener is no longer on the sill.

It is seated in the empty measured point.

Not tightened.

One turn short.

Kai says nothing.

Ade follows his line of sight.

His grip tightens around Kai.

Malik looks too.

The generator runs.

A child cries outside.

Normal sound.

Normal distance.

The fastener remains where none of them put it.

CUT TO BLACK.